  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Perindopril may be associated with Coronary Artery Disease through multiple indirect pathways. Perindopril is linked to raised serum uric acid, which is caused by Telmisartan and is itself treated for Coronary Artery Disease. Additionally, Perindopril is associated with serositis, which is caused by Enalapril and Timolol, both of which are used to treat Coronary Artery Disease. These pathways highlight the role of Perindopril in influencing physiological states that may contribute to the development of Coronary Artery Disease. The evidence is supported by a combination of causal relationships and shared biological mechanisms, such as the involvement of antihypertensive agents and metabolic pathways. However, the strength of the evidence is moderate, and the exact mechanisms by which Perindopril influences Coronary Artery Disease remain partially understood, necessitating further research to clarify the extent of its therapeutic and pathophysiological impact.",
